import "core"


# The default arrow. A name with no arguments applied is a value in its own right — the partial
  application `f` has before anything is given to it — so an arrow may be written from it.
  `f= f(b= 2, c= 3)` rewrites that bare `f`, and the `a` of `f(a= 1)` is applied on top of what the
  rewrite produced, reaching the full `f(a, b, c)`. So `b` and `c` are defaults, supplied when the
  call site does not give them. The defaults are distinct and non-zero, so the answer distinguishes
  a default that landed in the wrong slot.
  The same thing written under two names is `partialApply.ct`; what this file adds is that the name
  is the SAME on both sides, which is what makes it a default rather than a second function. That
  costs the form a self-reference at every stage: the arrow's body is built FROM the name the arrow
  rewrites, so without a rule saying otherwise the arrow is defined in terms of itself. The rule is
  that an arrow does not rewrite the base of its own body — `TypeCheck.TypeUtils.dropSelfBaseReach`
  for the inferred type (whose result would otherwise never leave `TopType`), and the `ObjSrc` gate
  in `TreeBuild.baseBuildMeta` for the tree built from it (which would otherwise recurse without
  bound at eval, since each eval-time rebuild starts a fresh `VisitedArrows`).
f(a -> Integer, b -> Integer, c -> Integer) -> Integer= a + b + c
f= f(b= 2, c= 3)

main{io -> IO} -> (){IO} =
  #assert(test= f(a= 1) == 6)
  ()
